my last question before some current  events is uh let's say that  uh you know libertarian conservative uh  viewpoint in the 90s would you consider  yourself a libertarian a republican how  do you kind of label yourself if you had  to put yourself in a box today and like  did that change over time or has it  always been kind of the same box you  would have put yourself in throughout  your life  yeah i guess when i was in college uh  you sort of figure out where your  political views are and how you would  categorize them and and yeah i would say  i was more on the libertarian end  of the the spectrum you know during like  let's say the reagan era  the um  the the conservative movement was sort  of an uneasy  uh alliance of a few different groups  you had sort of free market types a lot  like milton friedman  uh you had  social conservatives and then you had  sort of anti-communist  uh you know cold warrior types and um  and i guess the group that i  identified myth with the most would have  been more on the free market economic  side but i also agree with kind of the  reaganite approach to peace through  strength but in any event um  and then you know after the cold war  ended i think the political categories  got reshuffled a little bit and um  and so i would say yeah my views today  have have evolved and they're probably a  little bit more in a populist  direction um and we can delve more more  into that  explain a little bit more well  so  um  if you look at what the big political  divide is in america if you actually  poll people about their political views  the number one predictor of where  someone's gonna fall in the political  spectrum is whether they have a college  degree or not interesting and so if you  actually look at the views of people who  are in the professional class i.e people  have a college degree  is radically different than the views by  and large of the working class which is  people with just say a high school  education  and that is the fundamental  division in this country now it's not  universally true there are plenty of  people with a college degree like me who  have more let's say working class views  and then vice versa so but by and large  that's the division and there's a whole  host of issues  where uh the the professional class is  very much out of touch with what the  working class thinks and  the uh the uh political scientist who  actually i think has developed this the  most is a guy named roy tashira  who everyone should really check out his  sub stack if you're interested in this  um but he's been doing work over the  last couple of years sort of documenting  this and showing the polling and so  forth  he remember he is the author back in the  early like 2002 of a book called the  coming or democratic majority maybe  emerging democratic majority  in which he predicted  that demographic trends would result  in the election of democratic presidents  as far as the eye could see  and he was then pretty much considered a  prophet because obama then got elected  in 2008 building the type of coalition  that tashira describes in his book  interesting um  but lately over the last couple of years  and tashira is a democrat wants  democrats to win he's been warning the  party you have gotten way too far left  on sociocultural issues like for example  censorship as one of many  um and the reason is because you stop  being a working-class party you are now  a professional class party you cater to  the views  of uh of professional elites  uh and  but but but but you're speaking to  yourself you're speaking increasingly to  an echo chamber because you are out of  touch with  the rest of the country and so for  example  he very much predicted something like  the election of uh glen younkin in  virginia remember  this is a state that is pretty much a  blue state now virginia  biden won the state by 10 points in 2020  and one year later youngkin wins by two  or three points so 12 13 point swing why  on 